## Future Connection to Interrupt Controller

The timer is designed to eventually connect to the Interrupt Controller from the previous project.

The future architecture will be:

    Timer
      │
      │ IRQ
      ▼
    Interrupt Controller
      │
      │ IRQ_OUT
      ▼
    RISC-V CPU

This means that the timer can periodically interrupt the CPU.

For example:

    Timer reaches period
          ↓
    Timer generates IRQ
          ↓
    Interrupt Controller
          ↓
    CPU receives interrupt
          ↓
    CPU runs interrupt handler

This is an important mechanism for operating systems.

---

## Future Connection to Wishbone

The timer will eventually become a memory-mapped peripheral.

The CPU could then configure it through the Wishbone bus.

A future register map could look like:

| Address  | Register        | Description          |
|----------|-----------------|----------------------|
| "0x4000" | "TIMER_CONTROL" | Enable/disable timer |
| "0x4004" | "TIMER_PERIOD"  | Timer period         |
| "0x4008" | "TIMER_COUNTER" | Current counter      |
| "0x400C" | "TIMER_STATUS"  | Timer status         |

However, these registers are not part of the current project yet.

The current timer should remain standalone.

---

## Why the Timer will matter for Linux

Operating systems need a reliable source of periodic timing.

Linux uses timers for many tasks, including:

- Process scheduling
- Timekeeping
- Delays
- Software timers
- Periodic system tasks

A simplified example is:

    Timer
      ↓
    Interrupt
      ↓
    CPU
      ↓
    Linux kernel
      ↓
    Scheduler

The timer therefore becomes an important component of the final Linux-capable SoC.

